  72:     // We keep track of all the focus requesters in this map, with their clientId as the key.
  73:     // This is used both for focus dispatch and death handling
  74:     // Note that the clientId reflects the AudioManager instance and listener object (if any)
  75:     // so that one app can have more than one unique clientId by setting up distinct listeners.
  76:     // Because the listener gets only LOSS/GAIN messages, this is important for an app to do if
  77:     // it expects to request focus concurrently for different USAGEs so it knows which USAGE
  78:     // gained or lost focus at any given moment.  If the SAME listener is used for requests of
  79:     // different USAGE while the earlier request is still in the focus stack (whether holding
  80:     // focus or pending), the new request will be REJECTED so as to avoid any confusion about
  81:     // the meaning of subsequent GAIN/LOSS events (which would continue to apply to the focus
  82:     // request that was already active or pending).
  83:     private final Map<String, FocusEntry> mFocusHolders = new ArrayMap<>();
  84:     private final Map<String, FocusEntry> mFocusLosers = new ArrayMap<>();
